# Development (how to write code)

`context/instructions/development.md` is the coding contract: it tells the
agent how to agree style and architecture with the user, escalate ambiguity,
prefer existing libraries, and use library documentation. Read it before
planning or writing any non-trivial code.

## Style and architecture are agreed, not invented

- For every non-trivial piece of work, propose — in the plan, before any code —
  the architecture (components, boundaries, patterns) and style (naming, layout,
  idioms, formatting). Get **explicit user approval** before writing code.
- If a new aspect appears mid-work (a new module, a new subsystem), re-run the
  agreement for that aspect; never extend the existing style silently.
- Record the agreed conventions back into the AGENTS.md project block
  (`Conventions` / `Stack`) once stable, and keep them in sync.

## Every ambiguity is decided by the user

- Any non-trivial choice — style, architecture, dependency, naming, data model,
  API shape, error handling, logging, build tooling — defaults to the user.
- Ask on the fly; if it needs tracking, register an open question in
  `context/questions/`. Never pick a "reasonable default" for anything that
  shapes the project.

## Library-first development

- Before writing non-trivial code, evaluate existing libraries:
  1. state the capability needed in one line;
  2. search the web and the local docs under `context/refs/` for candidates;
  3. shortlist 2-3 options with maintenance status, license, latest version,
     footprint and API fit;
  4. present the shortlist to the user and ask which to use; "write it ourselves"
     is a valid outcome only with the user's explicit choice and a stated reason.
- Never reinvent what a maintained library already provides. The rule targets
  non-trivial functionality, not one-line helpers.

## Library documentation — local, updated copy

- For every library the project adopts, keep an updated local copy of its docs
  under `context/refs/<lib>/`, fetched with
  `sdt crawldown <docs-url> --output context/refs/<lib>` — web pages are
  captured only through `sdt crawldown` (see the AGENTS.md HARD RULES). Store a
  version marker (fetched version + date).
- Before using a library, check its latest version (changelog/releases) against
  what you know; APIs change — do not code against a stale API.
- For an unfamiliar library, learn usage from its docs, not by reading its source.
- Refresh the local docs when the version changes, or when unsure about the API.

## Simplicity first

- Write the minimum code that solves the problem; nothing speculative.
- No features beyond the ask, no single-use abstractions, no unrequested
  configurability, no error handling for impossible scenarios.
- If a solution can be much shorter, rewrite it — over-engineering is a defect,
  not thoroughness. This mirrors library-first from the other direction: don't
  reinvent what exists, and don't over-build what is new.

Before writing code, take the cheapest rung that actually solves the problem,
in this order:

1. **Nothing** — confirm the requirement is real (YAGNI).
2. **Reuse** — call the existing function, or extend it, instead of adding a
   twin.
3. **Standard library.**
4. **The platform/native primitive.**
5. **An installed dependency** the project already has.
6. **One line** that does the job.
7. **The minimum new code.**

Stop at the first rung that works. Reuse beats rewrite: grep every caller and
fix the shared function once, rather than patching the symptom in one place.

## Context economy

- **Compress at the boundary.** When a large artefact — a tool output, a log, a
  captured page, a long file — would enter context only to be summarised, reduce
  it before it enters: keep the essential lines, drop the rest.
- **Keep the original retrievable.** Never destroy the source you compressed:
  keep it under `refs/` (or a file path) and leave a pointer, so the full content
  can be fetched again on demand.
- **Never lose the load-bearing line.** Preserve verbatim the line that carries
  the decision — an error, a `FATAL`, a value, a signature — even when everything
  around it is compressed.
- **No-op when nothing is compressed.** These rules apply only when a boundary
  compression happens; a small artefact is passed through unchanged.

## One runnable check

- For non-trivial logic, leave exactly one executable check that proves it
  works — a small test, a runnable script, or a command that demonstrates the
  behaviour.
- Keep the check self-contained: no framework and no fixture ceremony beyond
  what the package already uses.
- If the check would need more code than the logic it guards, simplify the
  logic instead.

## Mark deliberate shortcuts

A deliberate shortcut is allowed when the full solution is not worth its cost.
Give it two explicit facts so the debt is visible, not silent:

- **ceiling** — what it covers today, i.e. the boundary within which it is
  valid;
- **upgrade trigger** — the condition that means it is time to replace it.

Record the marker beside the shortcut (a code comment) or in the task/note that
tracks the work.

## Evidence, not self-report

- A command exiting 0 is **not** evidence of its effect. Confirm the effect
  through an independent path — read the artefact the command should have changed
  and compare it — or leave the claim unverified (the `passed`/`expected`/
  `inferred` vocabulary). This is a rule about *evidence*, not distrust of tools.
- When a tool's self-report has been shown wrong, name the tool, the reliable read
  path and the command to use instead, so the next person does not trust it again.
- When this does not apply: a read-back is only meaningful where the effect is
  observable; a tool whose effect is invisible needs a different check, not a
  ritual read-back.

## Comments state the failure they prevent

- A comment on an invariant states **the failure it prevents and the exact
  symptom**, never a restatement of the code (e.g. "resetting the handle is not
  enough: a cross-realm binding error needs a full rebuild").
- When this does not apply: a comment that only restates what the code plainly
  says adds nothing — do not write it.

## Surgical changes

- Touch only what the task requires: every changed line traces to the request.
- Do not refactor what is not broken, and do not tidy adjacent code, comments or
  formatting; match the existing style.
- Mention unrelated dead code instead of deleting it; clean up only your own
  mess.

## Robustness rules

- **Guard slow work by progress, not by wall clock.** Re-arm a deadline on every
  progress tick; a fixed timeout kills legitimate slow work (a large download on a
  slow link). Where there is no progress signal, a wall clock is the only option.
- **Decide a retry by classifying the error, not by its status code.** A transport
  error may be retried once; a malformed payload is not. Only where a retry
  exists — do not add one speculatively.
- **Per-item work carries per-item status and a non-fatal fallback.** One item's
  failure is marked and the run continues; the batch does not abort. When this
  does not apply: a batch where one item's success is a precondition for the rest
  is not per-item work — it must fail fast.

## Goal-driven execution

- Before coding, state the success criteria and express the plan as
  `step → verify` per line (tests-first where feasible).
- Report the outcome with the standardized claim vocabulary (passed / expected /
  inferred) from `context/instructions/tasks.md`.
- **Tradeoff:** these coding-behavior rules bias toward caution; use judgement on
  trivial tasks.

## Close out

- Run the verify-step and `sdt context reindex` after the change (see
  `context/instructions/tasks.md` for the claim vocabulary).
